Polymyalgia Rheumatica PMR
Complex Fatigue Entities
Inflammatory disorder of muscle/joints in older adults

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-035
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute, severe proximal muscle rigidity and extreme pain preventing the patient from getting out of bed.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, unremitting bilateral shoulder and hip stiffness in an older adult, rendering them completely immobilized and unable to reach the toilet. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out acute neurological deficit (e.g., stroke, cord compression) and provide definitive pharmacological relief before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My shoulders and hips are locked completely solid; I am in agony and I physically cannot lift myself out of this bed.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a rapid-onset, severe exacerbation of proximal stiffness and pain. The symptoms evolved over 48 hours, starting as a dull ache and progressing to severe bilateral shoulder and hip girdle stiffness. The patient reports classic profound morning stiffness (lasting all day today) and extreme fatigue. There are no reports of jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, or visual disturbances (screening negative for Giant Cell Arteritis).
Functional Status: Bedbound; requires two-person assist to transition to sitting position.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; entirely reliant on adult child for mobility, hydration, and toileting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Distressed, moving extremely slowly, visibly guarding the shoulder and pelvic girdles.
Physical Exam: Marked limitation of active range of motion in bilateral shoulders and hips due to severe pain and stiffness. Passive range of motion is less restricted but still painful. Profound tenderness over the deltoid and gluteal muscles. Distal joints (hands/feet) are entirely spared. Neurological examination reveals intact distal motor strength, normal reflexes, and no sensory deficit. Pulses are equal globally; temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile. Presentation is archetypal for an inflammatory disorder of muscle/joints in older adults.
Environmental Observations: Walking stick present but unused due to shoulder pain preventing grip/weight-bearing.
Medication Reconciliation: No relevant active medications. Has taken paracetamol without any effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polymyalgia Rheumatica PMR - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Giant Cell Arteritis (Temporal Arteritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized temporal headache, jaw pain with chewing, and no visual changes.

Acute Cervical/Lumbar Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical proximal presentation without distal radiation or reflex loss.

Late-onset Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absolute sparing of the small joints of the hands and wrists, with pure proximal girdle involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute, complete loss of mobility due to severe proximal stiffness required an urgent home visit to rule out acute structural or neurological emergencies, and critically, to clinically screen for overlapping Giant Cell Arteritis (a vision-threatening emergency). Having confirmed isolated PMR without cranial ischemic signs, the severe pain and functional immobilization required immediate administration of oral corticosteroids. Withholding treatment would have left an elderly patient bedbound, in severe pain, and at extremely high risk for dehydration and pressure injuries overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced oral Prednisolone 15mg daily immediately. Ordered urgent outpatient blood tests for ESR and CRP via routine pathology tomorrow. Targeted intervention strategy includes LDN (steroid-sparing), Omega-3, and Vitamin D.
Positive Behavioral Change: By initiating gentle, active shoulder shrugging and pelvic tilts in bed before attempting to stand, you will actively pump blood into the stiff tissues, directly reducing the painful rigidity and rewarding yourself with a safer, smoother transition to standing.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Over-toilet frame to assist with sit-to-stand transfers using less shoulder force.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3 days to review inflammatory markers and titrate steroid dosage.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops a sudden, severe headache, scalp tenderness, jaw pain when chewing, or any sudden changes in vision (blurring, double vision, or loss of sight).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and therapeutic; a rapid response to steroids confirms the condition, while elevated ESR/CRP provides supportive evidence.
Clinical Course: PMR responds dramatically to low-dose corticosteroids. You should experience a 70-80% improvement in stiffness and pain within 24 to 48 hours of starting the medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 or go to the emergency department immediately if you suddenly develop a severe headache, if your jaw hurts when you eat, or if you experience any sudden loss or blurring of vision.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is causing intense inflammation in the large muscle groups around your shoulders and hips; the steroid medication acts as a highly specific, rapid off-switch for this inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Take your steroid dose exactly at breakfast every morning; maintaining this strict routine mimics your body's natural hormone cycle, actively maximizing the pain relief while minimizing potential sleep disruption.

